[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115083,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Desmoplastic fibroma of bone (DFB) is a locally aggressive fibroblastic/myofibroblastic tumour composed of benign spindle cells embedded in a collagenous background, mimicking desmoid-type fibromatosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115084,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8823/1 Desmoplastic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115085,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F7B &amp; XH6YK5 Neoplasms of uncertain behaviour of bone and articular cartilage &amp; Desmoplastic fibroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115086,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> desmoid tumour of bone; fibromatosis of jaw.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115087,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115088,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The jaws are a site of predilection. In the jaws, 82% of DFBs affect the mandible, 70% affecting the posterior body and angle {16360612; 25113037; 27816276; 32215029; 33204607}. The remainder involve the maxilla, usually anteriorly.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115089,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most individuals (66%) present with asymptomatic swelling or facial asymmetry. Other signs and symptoms include pain (15%), trismus with limited mouth opening (11%), mobile teeth (7%), infection (3%), and bleeding (3%) {16360612; 25113037; 27816276; 32215029; 33204607}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Radiography shows a well-defined radiolucency without mineralization, either unilocular or multilocular, with pushing borders, expansion, cortical erosion, tooth displacement, and root resorption, often with soft tissue extension {16360612; 25113037; 27816276; 32215029; 33204607}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115090,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DFB presents over a wide age range (0.5&ndash;70&nbsp;years) {16360612; 25113037; 27816276; 32215029; 33204607}, with 35% occurring in the first decade of life and almost 70% occurring before the age of 30&nbsp;years (mean age at diagnosis: 20&nbsp;years). DFB in the jaws has a slight female predilection (M:F&nbsp;ratio: 1:1.3) and is more prevalent in non-Hispanic White people (51%) than in people of African (24%), Hispanic (16%), or Asian (9%) descent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115091,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n<h5>Genetic factors</h5>\r\n<p>DFB occurs in tuberous sclerosis, more commonly involving the maxilla (60%) {15619647; 31078508}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115092,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115093,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DFB is a firm white tumour with a coarse, whorled architecture; focal myxoid areas; and no necrosis. Soft tissue extension may be present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115094,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "<p>DFB is composed of uniform benign spindle cells with slender, tapering nuclei, arranged in intertwining fascicles, without atypia or pleomorphism and with only rare mitoses. The cells often have indistinct cytoplasmic borders that blend with adjacent collagen, which can become keloid-like. Myxoid areas, cysts, and osteoclasts are present rarely. Thin-walled vessels interposed between spindle cell fascicles may be present. Because of the infiltrative margin, entrapped residual bone trabeculae may be present.</p>\r\n<h5>Immunohistochemistry</h5>\r\n<p>DFBs express vimentin (92%), SMA (80%), MSA (63%), and S100 (7%), and they have low Ki-67 expression {16360612; 25113037; 27816276; 32215029; 33204607}. Almost all DFBs lack nuclear &beta;-catenin expression; only 2&nbsp;cases with <em>CTNNB1</em> mutation have been reported.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Differential diagnosis is by exclusion of desmoid fibromatosis (nuclear &beta;-catenin; <em>CTNNB1</em> or <em>APC</em> mutation), fibrous dysplasia (<em>GNAS</em> mutation), low-grade central osteosarcoma (MDM2, CDK4), low-grade myofibroblastic sarcoma (SMA, desmin, nuclear &beta;-catenin), synovial sarcoma (<em>SS18</em> rearrangement), spindle cell rhabdomyosarcoma of the jaw (<em>TFCP2</em> rearrangement), and myoepithelial tumours (S100, CAM5.2; <em>EWSR1</em> rearrangement, SMARCB1 [INI1] loss).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-10T12:47:57"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 115095,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 174,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-09-28T12:37:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
